A||||7||Shiner||Raditor Fan||||1
Z||000000||Shiner||10-31-1999||01:38 AM||||My radiator fan has not been working.  He verified that the fan motor was good, the fusible link is good, the coolant temperature switch is getting power.  The fan relay was getting power also, but we could not close it with 12 volt DC.  So I replaced it.  I had the car idling to see if the fan would come on.  I got it up past the middle click (forget what temp it said, it's a GT guage if that makes any difference) but the fan would not kick on.  I didn't want to risk overheating the engine, so I shut her down.  What temp should the relay click, and turn on the fan?  And approximatly where should the needle be when I should kick in?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Greg Piet||10-31-1999||02:13 AM||||On my 85 SE the fan turns on at a ridiculous temp.<P>Grounding the green/white wire coming from the temp switch in the engine will test the circuit.  <BR>I ended up putting a switch in next to the lighter in the car which would either turn on the diagnostic mode for the ECM, or turn on the fan.  Just splice a wire from connector D1 (official Fiero service manual) at the wire junction terminal between the firewall and the driver compartment.  This wire is also a green/white wire then completing that wire to ground will kick on the fan.  <P>I asked a while abck about a lower temp fan switch, and there are variable temp switches out there, I think JC whitney sels one for 25 bucks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula||10-31-1999||08:16 AM||||Turn your AC on to see if it works||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-31-1999||11:37 AM||||the single speed fan turns on at 235F or whenever the AC is on.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Linx||10-31-1999||09:43 PM||||yeah- turn on the AC.  The fan should come on.  Also, put your ECM into diagnostic mode (by grounding the terminal on the aldl connector); this will force the fan on, too.<P>If these make the fan work, I'll betcha it's the temp sensor.  It's easy to forget there's more than one sensor.  One for the gauge, and one for the ECM.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Boxdin||10-31-1999||10:42 PM||||i like the idea of a manual switch for the fan, in adition to its automatic capability. How is it I do that on an 88 Formula?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||11-01-1999||02:50 AM||||I dont think diag mode turns on the fan.<BR>I may be wrong<P>as far as wiring a switch for an 88 gt I would guess it is similiar to the method i described above ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||11-01-1999||05:57 AM||||You say your relay wouldn't close with 12VDC  the relay is supposed to close with a connection to ground. Try taking the top off of the relay and closing the contacts by hand and see if the fan works. Also make sure you are checking the right temp switch- there are three of them.||||reg||1
